{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\n`list_tickets` against this store can terminate with `MCP request failed: Transport closed`\nand no structured error payload — the caller cannot tell whether the query was invalid, the\nserver failed, or the response was simply too large.\n\n## Proven root cause\n\nUnfiltered `tools/call list_tickets {}` against the deployed endpoint returned `http=200`,\n**94,541,551 bytes over 177.29 seconds**, in one well-formed SSE frame. **The server does not\nfail.** The client abandons a 94.5 MB / 177 s response, and how it abandons is client-specific\n(Copilot CLI: `Transport closed`; a 30 s-budget client: timeout; a third: 219,815 chars spilled\nto disk). None can distinguish \"too big\" from \"server died\", because the server offers no bound\nand no structured signal.\n\n## Reconciliation with `unreal-milky-sloth` (`6fe7-956f-4901-45cf`) — read before fixing\n\nThat sibling **REFUTED the response-size hypothesis and was right to on its evidence**: its\nfailing call was 50,532 bytes while another client succeeded at ~219,000 (\"size does not\ndiscriminate\"), its symptom was `TypeError: Do not know how to serialize a BigInt` — a type\nerror, not a size or timeout error — and post-fix it live-validated `ready_tickets` returning\n939,719 characters successfully.\n\nBoth findings stand, because they are **different failures**. That ticket: the server emitted\nbare 19-digit ints and a JS client threw while parsing; fixed, and `js_safe_result` now quotes\nthem. This ticket: measured **after** that fix shipped, the payload parses cleanly and is\n94.5 MB — not a serialization defect, an unbounded payload.\n\nSo the **219,815-character datum is NOT evidence for this ticket's cause and is not used as\nsuch**; at 4.3x a failing call it discriminates nothing, exactly as the sibling argued. The\ndiscriminating evidence is the post-fix 94.5 MB / 177 s measurement, which BigInt cannot\nexplain. The other original candidates are closed: BigInt **REJECTED** (the call completes and\nparses); deploy cutover **REJECTED** (reproducible three days after the 18:54:29Z replacement,\nfrom a session never orphaned).\n\n## The guard that already exists, and why it is not enough\n\nNot greenfield. `list_tickets` is already lean-by-default (`full: bool = False`), dropping\n`description`/`comments` — but that saves only 39% on this store, because **88% of the lean\npayload is base64 DSSE signature material** (`authorship_ledger` 59%, `attestations` 23%,\n`signature` 6%) that no list caller consumes: 15,548 bytes per row, 1,726 without it.\n`ready_tickets` has **no lean mode and no limit at all** (61 rows, 693,556 bytes). The one\npayload bound that exists — `_cap_workflow_payload`, 90,000 bytes, \"under the client's ~25K-token\nbudget\" — is wired only to `get_workflow_status`/`get_workflow_result`. Full measurement tables\nare in the Phase 4 comment.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] Cause identified and explicitly reconciled with both sibling tickets (above).\n\n- [ ] **One shared lean projection.** A single `lean_projection` over `LEAN_OMITTED_FIELDS`\n      (`_engine_support/reads.py`) is the only spelling of the lean row, dropping\n      `authorship_ledger`/`attestations`/`signature`/`keyring` as well as the two bodies, called\n      by BOTH backends: `list_states` and `_snapshot/ticket_view.py::TicketView.list_by_query`.\n      `ready_tickets` cannot reach it through the library (`rebar.ready` -> `ready_states` ->\n      `public_state` has no `include_body`/`full` plumbing) and this plan deliberately does NOT\n      add that plumbing; the MCP tool applies the same function at the tool layer\n      (`_mcp_reads._discovery_rows`). `full=True` restores the previous bytes exactly.\n\n- [ ] **Bounded, measured at a named seam.** Both list tools bound at the same 90,000-byte\n      `_WORKFLOW_TOKEN_BUDGET_BYTES`. Raw reducer rows under-estimate the real payload, so the\n      bound runs INSIDE the tool body after `TicketStateOut.model_validate` and CALLS\n      `js_safe_result` on those rows itself, rather than waiting for the error guard, which\n      applies that transform only after the body returns. Same pure function, same rows — so\n      measured bytes equal emitted bytes while the raise point stays where a structured error\n      is still deliverable.\n\n- [ ] **Structured refusal, right exception TYPE, closed vocabulary.** Raises a\n      `rebar._errors.RebarError` — the type matters, not just the attribute: `_envelope_error`\n      only envelopes a known rebar exception type, so a plain `Exception` carrying `.error_code`\n      is dropped and the caller gets an unstructured failure. Carries\n      `error_code=\"response_too_large\"`, naming match count, byte size, budget and a remedy.\n      Never a partial list; never a bare transport close. The value is added to\n      `rebar.KNOWN_ERROR_CODES` and `tests/unit/api_surface_baseline.json`. `error_code_for`\n      needs no change (step 5 returns a non-empty `exc.error_code`); no schema change\n      (`error_envelope.error` is an open string, not an enum).\n\n- [ ] **The remedy must be one the REFUSED TOOL can execute.** `list_tickets` accepts six\n      filters; **`ready_tickets` accepts only `sort` and `full` — no filters at all**, so a\n      shared \"narrow with has_tag\" message names arguments it rejects. `ready_tickets`' refusal\n      points at `next_batch(epic_id)` instead. Its measured headroom is ~1.7% (see comment), so\n      refusing is a near-term state and the remedy has to work.\n\n- [ ] **Any surface whose DEFAULT output narrows** is recorded as a pre-1.0 breaking change in\n      `docs/release-notes.md` and `CHANGELOG.md`, with measured before/after and the opt-out.\n\n- [ ] **Regression coverage asserts the error's CONTENTS**, not merely that a refusal occurred:\n      `error_code`, match count, byte size, budget value, remedy. Plus an under-budget list\n      returned whole, and the regression window — rows whose RAW size is under budget while the\n      wire payload is over must still be refused.\n\n- [ ] **LIVE VALIDATION — REQUIRED TO CLOSE.** `list_tickets` and `ready_tickets` through the\n      deployed endpoint return results (or a structured limit error) with no transport close.\n      **Auth precondition:** the endpoint is behind the OAuth gate, so the client MUST present a\n      valid bearer token; a `401`/`403` is a FAILED validation, not an environment excuse.\n      Evidence must be OBSERVED against the deployed server and recorded in a comment naming the\n      tools, arguments and actual response. A passing local test does NOT satisfy this, and\n      neither does a merged change: the container must have redeployed and served the call."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787954227709798001,"uuid":"d4b1de39-4251-40dd-b9c0-099f28af7c05"}